WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.980
People are central to Good Manufacturing
Practice. Every person whose activities

2
00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:07.630
can affect product quality
needs to understand

3
00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:10.700
their assigned responsibilities. Clear

4
00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:13.750
reporting lines, appropriate
staffing, and suitable

5
00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:17.540
supervision support
consistent execution. Staff

6
00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:20.310
must follow approved
procedures, record work

7
00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:24.160
accurately, and report
abnormal conditions.

8
00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:28.380
GMP is not only the responsibility
of the quality department; it

9
00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:31.380
depends on coordinated
action across the site.

10
00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:37.160
Personnel should receive initial and
continuing training relevant to their duties.

11
00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:40.590
Training may include GMP
principles, job-specific

12
00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:44.360
procedures, hygiene,
safety and contamination

13
00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:46.730
control. The organization should evaluate

14
00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:49.640
whether personnel can
perform assigned tasks

15
00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:54.640
competently, using methods appropriate
to the task and risk. A training

16
00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:57.350
attendance record alone
does not necessarily

17
00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:01.000
demonstrate practical
competence. Retraining may

18
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:05.290
be needed when procedures change
or performance gaps are identified.

19
00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:11.470
Personal hygiene helps reduce the risk
of contamination. Personnel should follow

20
00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:16.950
site rules for health reporting, hand
hygiene, protective clothing, and restrictions

21
00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:22.470
on items brought into manufacturing
areas. Hygiene expectations depend on the

22
00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:26.050
activity and contamination
risk. For example,

23
00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:28.990
personnel handling exposed product require

24
00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:34.910
controls appropriate to the manufacturing
environment. Site procedures should clearly explain

25
00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:37.830
what to do when a hygiene
concern is identified.

26
00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:43.110
Gowning is a controlled process,
not simply putting on a garment.

27
00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:46.210
The clothing type, gowning
sequence, changing-room

28
00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:48.550
arrangements and entry controls depend

29
00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:52.990
on the area classification and
operations. Personnel should be trained in

30
00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:55.630
the approved gowning
procedure and any applicable

31
00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:58.610
qualification requirements. In sterile

32
00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:01.330
manufacturing, gowning
controls are particularly

33
00:02:01.330 --> 00:02:05.110
important. This
introductory lesson does not

34
00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:09.710
replace detailed aseptic gowning
qualification or Annex One training.

35
00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:15.290
Personnel behaviour can affect contamination
control even when equipment and rooms

36
00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:18.130
are qualified. Follow approved movement

37
00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:21.810
restrictions, material
flows and entry rules.

38
00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:24.750
Avoid unnecessary
activity and interactions

39
00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:27.510
that could disturb
controlled conditions. Do

40
00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:30.430
not touch exposed product-contact surfaces

41
00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:33.490
without appropriate
controls. If an unexpected

42
00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:38.430
event occurs, stop or secure the
activity as required and follow

43
00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:43.410
the site escalation procedure. Good
habits help sustain a controlled state.

44
00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:50.290
Contamination and mix-up risks can arise
from incorrect materials, poor segregation,

45
00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:54.190
inappropriate handling,
and unclear identification.

46
00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:56.490
Personnel should verify
material and equipment

47
00:02:56.490 --> 00:02:59.450
status before use, follow line-clearance

48
00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:02.230
procedures where
applicable, and respect area

49
00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:05.070
and campaign controls. Labels and records

50
00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:08.590
should support identification
and traceability.

51
00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:12.890
When an item or status is
uncertain, do not guess; seek

52
00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:16.620
clarification and document the
concern according to procedure.

53
00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:22.340
Supervisors should ensure that personnel
understand their tasks and that required

54
00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:27.780
controls are followed. GMP records
should identify who performed or checked

55
00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:32.660
an activity when required. Entries
must reflect the actual work and

56
00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:38.400
should not be backdated or fabricated.
Observation, coaching and periodic review

57
00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:43.580
can help identify training needs. A
culture that supports timely reporting

58
00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:48.290
of mistakes and near misses contributes
to product quality and patient protection.

59
00:03:48.990 --> 00:03:53.990
Imagine an operator entering a controlled
manufacturing area who notices that

60
00:03:53.990 --> 00:03:58.330
the required gowning step was missed.
The appropriate response is not

61
00:03:58.330 --> 00:04:03.130
to continue silently. The operator
should follow the site procedure, which

62
00:04:03.130 --> 00:04:08.390
may require stopping, leaving the area,
regowning, and reporting the event.

63
00:04:08.390 --> 00:04:12.570
Any potential impact on operations
or product quality must be assessed

64
00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:19.050
by authorized personnel. The key principle
is to identify, contain, document

65
00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:21.450
and address the issue
rather than conceal it.
